  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Thursday...\n\nA shortwave ahead of troughing over the Pacific NW is set to move\nthrough overnight tonight. This is expected to bring a dry cold\nfront through the region with breezy south to southeast winds\nahead of the front today across southeast MT. Expect gusts 20-35\nmph through the day, with potentially stronger northwest winds \n(35-45 mph) with the front across the region overnight. As a\nresult, conditions will be cooler for the weekend. Approaching\ntroughing is expected to keep southwest flow in place, moving into\nthe region Sunday into Monday. With ensembles still showing the\nenergy splitting as it moves into MT, weak forcing will yield\nlittle in the way of precipitation. Currently, area mountains and\nsoutheast MT are expected to benefit from this event. Amounts have\ntrended down and there is now about a 20-40% chance for a quarter\ninch of precipitation for area foothills and southeast MT. Area\nmountains have about a 50-70% chance for this same Sunday through\nMonday period. The snowfall forecast for the mountains has also\ndecreased with about a 40% chance for an inch at the highest\npeaks Monday into Tuesday morning. The Beartooth Pass now has \nless than a 10% chance for an inch. Following this system, the mid\nlevel pattern looks to become more zonal keeping seasonable\ntemperatures around with no obvious weather systems at this time.\n\nHigh temperatures are expected to be warmest today, in the 70s to\nlow 80s, cooling to the 60s and low 70s for the remainder of the\nperiod. TS",
